<question>{{ task }}</question>

## Commands
The corpus and its ontology are available only through these commands (each takes --help):
- `kbsearch "words" [-k {{ max_hits }}] [--paper ID]`: full-text search (BM25) over the passages. Put exact phrases in double quotes. Prints passage ids.
- `kbread ID [--section WORD]`: one passage by passage id, or a paper's metadata and passage list by paper id.
- `kbpapers [--venue V] [--year Y] [--title WORD] [--author NAME] [--count]`: papers by DBLP metadata.
- `kbfacts PAPER_ID`, `kbfacts --cited-by PAPER_ID`, `kbfacts --author NAME`: DBLP facts (title, author, year, venue) and citations between corpus papers.
- `onto [WORD] [--kind class|property]`: the classes and properties of the ontology the knowledge graph uses.
- `kbcheck`: checks answer.json.

Each command runs in a new shell; files you write in the current directory stay. The run ends after {{ step_limit }} replies (model calls), so search with several words at once and read only promising passages.

## How to reply
{% if textbased %}
Every reply is a short thought followed by exactly one bash command in a ```mswea_bash_command block. A reply without a command is rejected.
{% else %}
Every reply is a short thought followed by exactly one call of the bash tool with one command. A reply without a tool call is rejected, and after a few rejections in a row the run ends without an answer. Never write the answer as plain text: the answer exists only once answer.json is written with a command.
{% endif %}

## What to deliver
Write `answer.json` in the current directory:

    {"answer": "...", "triples": [{"subject": "...", "predicate": "...", "object": "...", "source_id": "...", "evidence": "..."}]}

- answer: 1 to 5 sentences that answer the question directly. Put the source id of each claim in square brackets after it, e.g. "... evaluates on BenchY [p99-s3-p2]."
- triples: the facts behind the answer, {% if max_triples %}at most {{ max_triples }}, {% endif %}one fact each.
  - subject: the paper id when the fact is about a paper.
  - predicate: a property printed by `onto` when one fits (for example authoredBy or usesDataset); otherwise a new lowerCamelCase relation name that names the relation, not the object.
  - object: a paper id, an author name, a value or a short noun phrase.
  - source_id: the passage id or the fact source id (from kbfacts) that states the fact.
  - evidence: a short exact quote from that source.
- If the corpus does not answer the question, say so in answer and give "triples": [].

Write the file with a heredoc (cat <<'EOF' > answer.json ... EOF), run `kbcheck`, fix what it reports, and then finish with exactly this command and nothing else:

    echo COMPLETE_TASK_AND_SUBMIT_FINAL_OUTPUT && cat answer.json
